r. **Why it absolutely does function this way in a real organ:** Researchers have watched this happen. In studies where they combine real-time MRI or ultrasound with pH probes and marker dyes, they see that: - The lower part of the stomach (the antrum) contracts powerfully in a ring, forcing the liquefied part of the chyme toward the closed pylorus. - Only a small squirt of liquid and very fine particles escape; the rest washes backward. - A distinct layer of more-liquid material is often pushed ahead of large solids, which are trapped. - If you tag a later drink with a different dye, it races ahead of the solid meal and leaves the stomach within minutes, while the tagged solids from the meal remain. That is not chaos; that’s an elegantly simple, physics-based sorting algorithm. It’s the same principle as panning for gold: sloshing a mixture so that the heavier, larger stuff gets separated from the fine, light stuff that washes out. No clock, no memory, just physical state. **So why does it not *feel* like chaos to your body?** Because your body’s consciousness isn’t wired to track pyloric output. As we discussed, you feel distension (fullness), not which items are passing the sphincter. The orderly, grinding sensation you feel—a steady, rhythmic churn—*is* the antral mill doing its systematic work. It doesn’t feel chaotic because it’s a consistent mechanical rhythm, and the result is a smooth, controlled release of chyme into the duodenum over hours, perfectly matched to how fast the intestines can handle it. That’s not “total chaos”; that’s homeostatic precision. You’re absolutely right that a real organ wouldn’t function as random chaos. That’s how we know the stomach doesn’t do that. It functions as a highly structured physical filter whose rule is simple and orderly: pass fine, retain coarse, try again. It’s just that the rule is based on current physical properties, not on order of entry. The fact that it challenges our everyday intuition about queuing doesn’t